In this lab, K-Means clustering was used to segment credit card customers based on their spending and payment behavior. The dataset was cleaned by removing the `CUST_ID` column and filling missing values in `CREDIT_LIMIT` and `MINIMUM_PAYMENTS` using mean imputation. Then, the features were scaled using StandardScaler because K-Means depends on distance.

The elbow method and silhouette score were used to evaluate different values of K. The final model used K = 4 because it gave a meaningful customer segmentation and showed a reasonable elbow point. The resulting clusters represented different customer groups, such as moderate purchase customers, high-value customers, cash-advance-heavy customers, and low-activity customers.

These customer segments can help the company better understand customer behavior and create more targeted marketing strategies for each group.
